{"content": [{"text": "I must not use tools for this task. Let me work it out carefully by hand using a DP over positions, tracking (last digit, running sum).\n\nI'll exploit symmetry. Define a DP where after placing some positions I track the distribution over (last digit $d$, sum $s$). Because of the structure, I'll track counts as polynomials in the sum.\n\nLet me set up the recurrence. Let $V_n(d, s)$ = number of valid length-$n$ strings ending in digit $d$ with sum $s$. The transition is $V_{n+1}(d, s) = \\sum_{d' \\ne d} V_n(d', s-d)$.\n\nA key simplification: by the symmetry $d \\to 9-d$, the whole problem (sum 30 over 7 digits, max sum 63, and 30 is symmetric-ish: complement maps sum $s$ to $63-s$). Sum 30 maps to 33 under complement, so not self-symmetric. I'll just track all digits.\n\nLet me track, at each step, the vector $V_n(d, \\cdot)$ as a generating function in $x$ (exponent = sum). Define $g_n^{(d)}(x) = \\sum_s V_n(d,s) x^s$. Let $T_n(x) = \\sum_{d=0}^9 g_n^{(d)}(x)$ be the total. Then:\n$$g_{n+1}^{(d)}(x) = x^d \\big(T_n(x) - g_n^{(d)}(x)\\big).$$\n\nInitial: $g_1^{(d)}(x) = x^d$, so $T_1(x) = \\sum_{d=0}^9 x^d = 1+x+\\dots+x^9$.\n\nI only need coefficients up to $x^{30}$. This is a lot of polynomial arithmetic by hand. Let me instead use the inclusion-exclusion compositions approach, which I can organize into a finite, checkable table.\n\n**Inclusion-exclusion over compositions of 7.**\n\nAnswer $= \\sum_{\\text{compositions }(b_1,\\dots,b_m)\\text{ of }7}(-1)^{7-m}\\,[x^{30}]\\prod_{j}f_{b_j}(x)$, where $f_b(x)=\\sum_{d=0}^9 x^{bd}$.\n\nSince $[x^{30}]\\prod f_{b_j}$ depends only on the **multiset** of block sizes, and the sign depends only on $m$ = number of blocks, I group by integer partitions of 7. For a partition $\\lambda$ of 7 into $m$ parts, the number of compositions (ordered arrangements) is $\\binom{m}{m_1,m_2,\\dots}$ (multinomial over the multiplicities of equal parts). Each such composition contributes the same $N(\\lambda)=[x^{30}]\\prod_j f_{b_j}$ and sign $(-1)^{7-m}$.\n\nSo: **Answer** $= \\sum_{\\lambda \\vdash 7}(-1)^{7-m(\\lambda)}\\,C(\\lambda)\\,N(\\lambda)$, where $C(\\lambda)$ = number of compositions with that multiset of parts.\n\nLet me enumerate partitions of 7, with $m$ = #parts, $C$ = #compositions, and sign $(-1)^{7-m}$.\n\n| $\\lambda$ | $m$ | $C$ | sign |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | 1 | 1 | $(-1)^6=+$ |\n| 6,1 | 2 | 2 | $(-1)^5=-$ |\n| 5,2 | 2 | 2 | $-$ |\n| 4,3 | 2 | 2 | $-$ |\n| 5,1,1 | 3 | 3 | $(-1)^4=+$ |\n| 4,2,1 | 3 | 6 | $+$ |\n| 3,3,1 | 3 | 3 | $+$ |\n| 3,2,2 | 3 | 3 | $+$ |\n| 4,1,1,1 | 4 | 4 | $(-1)^3=-$ |\n| 3,2,1,1 | 4 | 12 | $-$ |\n| 2,2,2,1 | 4 | 4 | $-$ |\n| 3,1,1,1,1 | 5 | 5 | $(-1)^2=+$ |\n| 2,2,1,1,1 | 5 | 10 | $+$ |\n| 2,1,1,1,1,1 | 6 | 6 | $(-1)^1=-$ |\n| 1,1,1,1,1,1,1 | 7 | 1 | $(-1)^0=+$ |\n\nLet me verify the composition counts $C(\\lambda)$ sum to $2^6=64$ (total compositions of 7): $1+2+2+2+3+6+3+3+4+12+4+5+10+6+1$. \n\nSum: $1+2+2+2=7$; $+3+6+3+3=15\\to22$; $+4+12+4=20\\to42$; $+5+10=15\\to57$; $+6=63$; $+1=64$. ✓ Good.\n\nNow I need $N(\\lambda)=[x^{30}]\\prod_j f_{b_j}(x)$ where $f_b(x)=1+x^b+x^{2b}+\\dots+x^{9b}$ = number of solutions to $\\sum_j b_j d_j = 30$ with each $d_j\\in\\{0,\\dots,9\\}$.\n\nLet me define helper: for given block sizes, count integer solutions $d_j \\in [0,9]$ to $\\sum b_j d_j = 30$.\n\n**Partition 7 (single block, $b=7$):** $7d=30$. No solution (30 not divisible by 7). $N=0$. Contribution: $+1\\cdot0=0$.\n\n**Partition 6,1:** $6d_1+d_2=30$, $d_1,d_2\\in[0,9]$. For each $d_1$: $d_2=30-6d_1\\in[0,9]$. $d_1=4\\Rightarrow d_2=6$ ✓; $d_1=5\\Rightarrow d_2=0$ ✓; $d_1=3\\Rightarrow12$ ✗; so $d_1\\in\\{4,5\\}$. $N=2$. Contribution $-2\\cdot2=-4$.\n\n**Partition 5,2:** $5d_1+2d_2=30$. $d_1\\in[0,9]$, need $30-5d_1$ divisible by 2 and in $[0,18]$. $30-5d_1$ even $\\Rightarrow d_1$ even. $d_1=0\\Rightarrow d_2=15$✗; $d_1=2\\Rightarrow d_2=10$✗; $d_1=4\\Rightarrow d_2=5$✓; $d_1=6\\Rightarrow d_2=0$✓; $d_1=8\\Rightarrow 30-40<0$✗. Also odd $d_1$ give odd RHS, no. $N=2$. Contribution $-2\\cdot2=-4$.\n\n**Partition 4,3:** $4d_1+3d_2=30$. $d_2$ such that $30-3d_2$ divisible by 4, in $[0,36]$. $30-3d_2\\equiv0\\pmod4$. $30\\equiv2$, $3d_2\\equiv2\\pmod4\\Rightarrow 3d_2\\equiv2\\Rightarrow d_2\\equiv2\\cdot3^{-1}$; $3^{-1}=3\\pmod4$, so $d_2\\equiv6\\equiv2\\pmod4$. $d_2\\in\\{2,6\\}$ (also $d_2=10$ excluded). $d_2=2\\Rightarrow4d_1=24\\Rightarrow d_1=6$✓; $d_2=6\\Rightarrow4d_1=12\\Rightarrow d_1=3$✓. $N=2$. Contribution $-2\\cdot2=-4$.\n\n**Partition 5,1,1:** $5d_1+d_2+d_3=30$, $d_2,d_3\\in[0,9]$ so $d_2+d_3\\in[0,18]$. $5d_1=30-(d_2+d_3)$. For each $d_1$, count pairs $(d_2,d_3)$ with $d_2+d_3 = 30-5d_1$ in range $[0,18]$. Let $t=30-5d_1$. Number of $(d_2,d_3)\\in[0,9]^2$ with sum $t$: it's $t+1$ for $0\\le t\\le9$, and $19-t$ for $10\\le t\\le18$, else 0.\n- $d_1=2$: $t=20$ → out of range (>18), 0.\n- $d_1=3$: $t=15$ → $19-15=4$.\n- $d_1=4$: $t=10$ → $19-10=9$.\n- $d_1=5$: $t=5$ → $5+1=6$.\n- $d_1=6$: $t=0$ → $0+1=1$.\n- $d_1\\le2$: $t\\ge20$, 0. $d_1\\ge7$: $t<0$, 0.\n\n$N=4+9+6+1=20$. Contribution $+3\\cdot20=+60$.\n\n**Partition 4,2,1:** $4d_1+2d_2+d_3=30$, all in $[0,9]$. For each $(d_1)$, then sum over $d_2$ of indicator that $d_3=30-4d_1-2d_2\\in[0,9]$.\nLet me iterate $d_1=0..7$ (since $4\\cdot8=32>30$).\nFor fixed $d_1$, let $R=30-4d_1$. Need $2d_2+d_3=R$, $d_2,d_3\\in[0,9]$. Count = number of $d_2\\in[0,9]$ with $d_3=R-2d_2\\in[0,9]$, i.e. $R-9\\le 2d_2\\le R$ and $d_2\\le9,\\ge0$.\n- $d_1=0$: $R=30$. $2d_2\\ge21\\Rightarrow d_2\\ge11$ → impossible (max $d_2=9\\Rightarrow2d_2=18$, $d_3=12$✗). Count 0.\n- $d_1=1$: $R=26$. Need $2d_2\\in[17,26]$, $d_2\\in[9,13]\\cap[0,9]=\\{9\\}$? $d_2=9\\Rightarrow d_3=26-18=8$✓. Check $d_2=9$ only (need $2d_2\\ge17$→$d_2\\ge8.5$→$d_2=9$; $\\le13$ ok). Count 1.\n- $d_1=2$: $R=22$. $2d_2\\in[13,22]$→$d_2\\in[7,11]\\cap[0,9]=\\{7,8,9\\}$. $d_2=7\\Rightarrow d_3=8$;8→6;9→4. Count 3.\n- $d_1=3$: $R=18$. $2d_2\\in[9,18]$→$d_2\\in[5,9]$ (since $\\ge4.5$). $d_